---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/business/intermediate/what-a-business-is">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate Business lesson and the natural first stop of the intermediate
    tier. The foundational <strong>unit economics</strong> lesson is the prerequisite: price
    above cost, contribution margin and the per-unit view are exactly the "capture" half of
    the machine this lesson assembles. Everything here also arms you for the sibling
    lessons on customers, operations and people.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define business activity through the factors of production and added value;</li>
    <li>classify businesses by sector and ownership, and read the sector drift of rich economies;</li>
    <li>distinguish horizontal, vertical and conglomerate mergers, and recite the short list of reasons firms fail;</li>
    <li>compare sole traders, partnerships and companies, and explain why limited liability unlocked modern capital markets;</li>
    <li>trace how revenue models shape behaviour and how objectives reorder with circumstances.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Strip away the logos and a business is <strong>a machine for creating and capturing
    value</strong> — and the two halves are separate skills. A business creates value when it
    makes something worth more to a customer than the inputs cost; it captures value when it
    keeps part of that surplus as price above cost. Many firms create value they never
    capture (inventions without patents, networks without monetisation — newspapers after the
    internet arrived); others capture value they never created (rents, lock-ins, tricks) —
    until customers find the exit. Everything else on this page — the legal forms, the
    sectors, the growth paths, the objectives — is machinery for doing those two things
    better and for longer.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="drag the divider — two halves, two skills" widget="compare" config={wbCreateCompare} />

  <Widget no="1" caption="create, capture, hypothesise, measure" widget="equationStep" config={whatCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four panels as a diagnostic, not a
    definition list. Panels 1 and 2 separate the two skills — creating value and capturing
    it — and most business failure is one of those halves missing. Panel 3 turns the whole
    firm into a testable hypothesis, and panel 4 warns that the scoreboard (profit) lags
    the decisions that produced it by years.
  </p>

  <h2>What business activity actually is</h2>
  <p>
    Economics gives the cleanest definition: business activity combines the <strong>factors
    of production</strong> — land (all natural resources), labour (human effort and skill),
    capital (machines, buildings, money deployed into production) and <strong>enterprise</strong>
    (the organising, risk-bearing factor that combines the other three) — to produce goods and
    services that satisfy human wants. Wants are effectively unlimited; resources are not;
    that scarcity is the reason <em>adding value</em> is the whole game. A café buys coffee
    beans, milk and labour for perhaps 1.20 and sells the cup for 3.50: the 2.30 of added
    value pays for the rent, the risk and the owner's living — and customers happily pay it
    because a convenient hot cappuccino at 8 a.m. is worth more to them than 3.50. Notice
    the direction: value is defined by the <em>customer's</em> willingness to pay, not the
    producer's cost or pride. Businesses fail when they reverse the arrow — building what
    they can make rather than what anyone wants.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="call the added value per cup" widget="estimate" config={wbCafeEstimate} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a business combines land, labour, capital and enterprise to add
    value, and the customer's willingness to pay — not the producer's cost — defines what
    that value is. Two sorting axes decide where any particular firm sits.
  </p>

  <h2>Classifying businesses</h2>
  <p>
    Two axes sort every business. The first is <strong>by sector</strong>: the
    <em>primary</em> sector extracts raw materials (farming, fishing, mining, oil); the
    <em>secondary</em> sector manufactures and constructs (steel mills, car plants, house
    builders); the <em>tertiary</em> sector provides services (retail, banking, transport,
    healthcare, software). The classification is more than taxonomy — it is history.
    Economies develop by moving through the sectors: in the UK around 1800, more than a
    third of employment was agricultural and manufacturing was surging; by 2023, agriculture
    employs under 1.5 per cent of UK workers and services employ over 80 per cent. This
    <em>deindustrialisation</em> — the drift from secondary to tertiary — is the standard
    story of rich economies, and it changes everything: what gets invested in, what skills
    schools teach, which towns thrive. The second axis is <strong>by ownership</strong>: the
    <em>private sector</em> (owned by individuals and shareholders) versus the
    <em>public sector</em> (owned by the state — schools, state railways, municipal water).
    The boundary is a live political question in every country: nationalise or privatise,
    and on what theory of who serves the public better?
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="file the industries by sector" widget="sort-buckets" config={wbSectorBuckets} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the tertiary sector is less important because services
    don't make anything." Measure by employment or GDP and the opposite is true in every
    developed economy — and a "service" like software logistics now directs the secondary
    sector itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Enterprise, growth and size</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>entrepreneur</strong> is the factor of production that takes the risk: they
    spot an opportunity, assemble the other factors, and bear the downside if it fails. The
    downside is real — depending on country and decade, roughly half of new businesses are
    gone within five years; survival past ten years is the minority outcome. Why do some
    grow and others stay small? Growth has two routes: <em>internal</em> (open more outlets,
    add products, enter new regions) and <em>external</em> (merge or acquire). Mergers come
    in three named flavours worth keeping straight: <strong>horizontal</strong> (two
    competitors combine — two bakeries; instant market share, and instant scrutiny from
    competition regulators), <strong>vertical</strong> (a firm buys a supplier or a
    distributor — backward integration toward raw materials, forward toward the customer;
    the motive is control of the chain, not share of a market) and
    <strong>conglomerate</strong> (unrelated businesses under one roof — pure
    diversification, increasingly out of fashion because investors can diversify themselves
    more cheaply, as the portfolio lesson showed). And many firms <em>stay</em> small on
    purpose: a niche too thin to attract giants, the owner's preference for control over
    scale, or the simple fact that some services (a barber, a bespoke joiner) cannot be
    mass-produced.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="pair each merger with its motive" widget="match" config={wbMergerMatch} />
  <p>
    The failures have a short, repeated list of causes, and it is worth memorising because
    it is the checklist investors and lenders actually run: <strong>lack of cash</strong>
    (profitable on paper, dead in practice — the timing gap between paying suppliers today
    and collecting from customers in sixty days), <strong>no market</strong> (built because
    it could be built), <strong>over-expansion</strong> (growing faster than working
    capital and management can carry — the classic way good restaurants die on opening
    their third branch), <strong>competition</strong> (someone does the same job cheaper or
    better), and <strong>key-person dependence</strong> (everything routes through one
    founder's head). Notice that four of the five are self-inflicted — which is why
    "survival" appears first in nearly every list of business objectives, and why seasoned
    operators respect boring cash discipline more than exciting strategy.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="one line of the failure checklist is wrong" widget="spot-error" config={wbFailureSpot} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a profitable business cannot fail." The failure list
  says otherwise: profit is an accounting verdict over a period, while suppliers, wages and
  rent fall due on dates. A firm that books healthy profit but collects from customers
  sixty days after paying its own suppliers can be profitable on paper and dead in
  practice — which is why "lack of cash" leads the list of killers.</p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="true or false — profit versus the calendar" widget="judge" config={wbProfitJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the entrepreneur bears the risk; roughly half of new firms are
    gone within five years, usually by self-inflicted causes, and legal form is the next
    lever — it decides who pays when things go wrong.
  </p>

  <h2>Types of business organisation</h2>
  <p>
    Legal form is a choice with consequences for liability, money-raising and control.
    The <strong>sole trader</strong> owns everything and owes everything — unlimited
    liability means the business's debts are the owner's personal debts, recoverable from
    the house and savings. Setup is trivial, control is total, capital is scarce, and death
    or burnout ends the firm. A <strong>partnership</strong> pools several owners' capital
    and skill — but general partners share unlimited liability too, which is why formal
    partnership agreements and limited partners exist. The <strong>company</strong>
    (corporation) is the decisive invention: it is a <em>separate legal person</em>, owning
    its own debts. <strong>Limited liability</strong> — shareholders lose only what they
    invested, never their homes — is what made modern capital markets possible: it lets
    strangers fund huge risky ventures without betting everything they own. The Dutch East
    India Company, chartered in 1602, was the first to issue shares to the general public;
    historians of capitalism have a decent case that this legal trick mattered more than
    any single technology. Private limited companies (shares traded by invitation only)
    suit family and founder-owned firms; <strong>public limited companies</strong> (freely
    traded shares, listing requirements, quarterly accountability) can raise vast capital —
    at the price of answering to the stock market every quarter. Other forms fill the gaps:
    <strong>franchises</strong> (buy a proven formula — McDonald's outlets are mostly
    franchisees' businesses), <strong>cooperatives</strong> (owned by members or workers,
    one member one vote), <strong>joint ventures</strong> (two firms pooling risk on one
    project) and <strong>public corporations</strong> (state-owned operators like national
    rail companies).
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="pick the legal skin, own the consequences" widget="branch" config={wbFormBranch} />

  <h2>Revenue models shape behaviour</h2>
  <p>
    Cross-cutting all of the legal forms is a quieter design choice: <em>how the firm
    charges</em>. The <strong>transaction model</strong> (pay per item) inherits an obsession
    with volume and repeat purchase. The <strong>subscription model</strong> (software,
    gyms, streaming) inherits an obsession with retention and churn — a subscriber paying
    10 a month who stays 30 months is a 300 customer, and the entire discipline of the firm
    bends toward keeping them. The <strong>advertising model</strong> (search, social,
    free-to-air media) means the customer is not the customer: the advertiser is, and the
    users are the audience being assembled and sold — which explains both the generosity of
    free products and their attention-harvesting design. The <strong>freemium model</strong>
    bets that a small converting minority subsidises a large free majority (roughly 2–5
    per cent conversion is typical; below that, free users are pure cost). Choose the
    revenue model and you inherit its incentives — often more decisively than the legal
    form shapes anything. A subscription newspaper investigates its government differently
    from an advertising-funded one; a freemium game designs retention hooks its
    transaction-priced rival would never build.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="file each revenue model by its obsession" widget="sort-buckets" config={wbRevenueBuckets} />

  <h2>Objectives and stakeholders</h2>
  <p>
    Why does the firm exist? The classic private-sector objectives are survival, profit,
    growth, and market share — and their <em>ordering changes with circumstances</em>. A
    startup in a cash crunch pursues survival (profit can wait); an established firm may
    sacrifice margin for share to build scale. Public-sector organisations typically pursue
    service quality, access and staying within budget — which is why "profit" is the wrong
    grading rubric for a public hospital, and "cost per successful treatment" a fairer one.
    Then the harder question: the firm is not one person with one goal but a web of
    <strong>stakeholders</strong> — owners, employees, customers, suppliers, lenders,
    government, the community — whose interests genuinely conflict. Cheaper suppliers
    delight shareholders and hurt suppliers; higher wages help staff and squeeze margins.
    Milton Friedman's famous 1970 answer was that the social responsibility of business is
    to increase its profits within the rules — managers spending shareholders' money on
    social causes are spending other people's money; the "stakeholder capitalism" answer is
    that firms ignoring employees, customers and communities will be punished by them over
    time, and the shareholder interest is long-run anyway. Notice the argument is really
    about <strong>time horizon and measurement</strong>: almost everyone agrees the firm
    should create value; the fight is over whose value, counted over how many years.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> stakeholder language means "profit doesn't matter."
    Read the actual positions: Friedman's case is a claim about managers' accountability,
    not a claim that customers and employees are irrelevant — no firm survives long by
    abusing them. The honest versions of both sides are about sequencing: serve
    stakeholders well <em>in order to</em> sustain profit, or maximise profit <em>subject
    to</em> not burning stakeholders. The operations rarely differ as much as the slogans.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A bakery buys the flour mill that supplies it; a different bakery merges with the bakery next door. Name both moves and the motive of each.</li>
    <li>Why did limited liability make modern capital markets possible?</li>
    <li>An advertising-funded game and a subscription game each have a million users. Why will the two products be designed differently?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Bakery-plus-mill is vertical integration — control of the
    chain, back toward raw materials. Bakery-plus-bakery is horizontal — two competitors
    combining for market share, which is exactly why competition regulators scrutinise it.
    (2) Shareholders lose only what they invested, never their homes — so strangers can fund
    huge risky ventures without betting everything they own; the Dutch East India Company's
    1602 public share issue built on exactly that foundation. (3) The revenue model sets the
    incentive: the subscription game must fight churn, so design bends toward retention,
    while the advertising game assembles attention and sells it, so design bends toward
    time-in-app — and a subscription newspaper investigates government differently from an
    advertising-funded one.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A business is a machine for creating value (worth more to customers than inputs cost) and capturing value (price above cost) — two separate skills.</li>
    <li>Factors of production: land, labour, capital, enterprise; scarcity is why adding value is the whole game.</li>
    <li>Economies drift from primary to secondary to tertiary; services dominate employment in every rich economy.</li>
    <li>About half of new firms are gone within five years — cash, no market, over-expansion, competition, key-person dependence; four of the five are self-inflicted.</li>
    <li>The company is a separate legal person; limited liability is what lets strangers fund risky ventures.</li>
    <li>Revenue models (transaction, subscription, advertising, freemium) decide what the firm obsesses over.</li>
    <li>Objectives reorder with circumstances — survival first in a crunch — and stakeholders' interests genuinely conflict.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The intermediate tier now fans out from this definition: <strong>customers and
    markets</strong> asks who the value should be created for, <strong>operations and supply
    chains</strong> how it gets produced at a cost the price supports, and <strong>people
    and organisations</strong> who does the work inside the machine.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three expert extensions. <strong>Value creation versus capture</strong> is the frame
    professionals actually use to diagnose failure: newspapers created colossal value
    (informing a nation) and captured almost none of it once the internet moved distribution
    to platforms that monetised the attention instead; airlines create miserable margins
    because what they offer is easy to copy and viciously competed — <em>the value you
    capture depends less on how much you create than on how hard you are to replace</em>,
    which is the strategy lesson's territory (Porter's five forces live there). The
    <strong>business model canvas</strong> (Osterwalder) makes the firm's hypothesis
    explicit — who pays, for what, through which channel, at what cost — and its real use
    is falsifiability: each box is a bet you can test. And remember that <strong>profit is
    a lagging signal</strong>: this quarter's cash reflects decisions made years ago, which
    is why investors judge companies on the trajectory of unit economics rather than the
    headline — and why a "profitable" firm can be dying while a "loss-making" one is
    compounding brilliantly. The open question running through all of it: as AI, platforms
    and state industrial policy blur the old boundaries between private and public,
    manufacturer and service, employee and contractor — the classification systems on this
    page are being rewritten in real time, and the firms that notice first set the terms.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Osterwalder &amp; Pigneur, Business Model Generation; Drucker, The Practice of Management; Friedman (1970) NYT essay; UK ONS employment data by sector. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
